<!--
    Native switch-label correctness, P9 emission; no option (strict bug fix).
    Authored x86-64 jump tables over signed/unsigned 8, 16, 32 and 64-bit
    selectors. Every table has arms returning 11 and 22, plus a
    default returning 33. Narrow inputs add 13 modulo their width, then
    zero-extend the guarded table index; signed and unsigned selectors have
    the same bits, but different declared types.
    Wide inputs add 13 modulo their width. An unsigned bound of 2 guards
    the table; its last entry shares the default result. The recovered
    selector retains its declared type.

    Before the fix all labels print positive: signed byte/short selectors
    cannot match them after C integer promotion. Signed wide labels also
    ignore their selector type. Unsigned high-bit labels must stay positive.
    signed16_load reads a signed short through a pointer, exercising an
    expression selector rather than a declared scalar. Lowered-switch
    recovery is off so these are native-table regressions. signed16_widen
    sign-extends before 32-bit arithmetic, so recovered label bits beyond
    the short selector width must be discarded before signed formatting.
-->
